# Read by ripgrep, and by the search tools of hosts that are built on it, in whatever tree
# holds this skill folder. A project install puts the skill inside the repository being
# reviewed (<repository>/.claude/skills/code-security-review, <repository>/.agents/skills/...),
# and a search of that repository would return lines from it: the planted fixture
# (tests/fixture/src/parse.c), and the rule tables and role files, which name every sink the
# rules look for. Measured with the skills then installed for two hosts: a
# hidden-aware search for a C library function returned 14 files of this skill per copy, and 44
# of the skills together, against 1 of the product.
#
# This hides nothing from a person or from a model that opens a file by its path. It keeps
# these out of searches of the code under review:
#   tests/        the planted fixture the selftests read
#   references/   the contract, the role files, the validator playbooks, the limits, the notes
#   scripts/      the tools, whose source holds the rule hits' vocabulary and the self-tests'
#                 synthetic vulnerable code
#   assets/       the rule tables and the corpora the rules were measured on
# SKILL.md stays visible. The patterns are anchored to the skill folder, so a `tests/` or a
# `scripts/` of the repository under review is still searched.
#
# The validators' own searches pass --no-ignore (so a repository cannot hide a caller from them
# with a committed .ignore), which is why they carry explicit leave-outs instead
# (`review_run.py unit-task` prints them: this skill's folder, every other installed skill and
# the run folders of the other skills). references/limitations.md says what that leaves.
# To search the skill itself, ask for it: rg --no-ignore PATTERN <skill folder>.
/tests/
/references/
/scripts/
/assets/
